00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:07.940
So, on to today's wonderful
speaker, Dianne Dwyer Modestini.

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:15.000
She's a Clinical Professor
in the Kress Program

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.700
in Paintings and Conservation Emerita

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.280
at NYU's Institute of Fine Arts.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:23.730
She's an internationally
renowned conservator of master

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:25.860
and 19th-Century paintings.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:30.090
She received a BA in Art
History from Barnard in 1968.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:32.730
After graduation, she
spent a year in Florence

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:34.380
where she studied Drawing at

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:37.230
the Academia de Bella Arte in Italian.

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:41.730
In later years, she was to
return often to this beloved city

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.310
where in 1996, she began and directed

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:48.150
the Restoration Program
at the Villa La Pietra.

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:49.260
She's on the Board

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:52.590
of the Foundation Roberto
Longhi in Florence.

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:55.440
she obtained an MA and a
Certificate of Advanced Study

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.890
in Art Conservation from the
State University of New York.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:01.770
She was appointed the Assistant
Conservator of paintings

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:04.680
at the Metropolitan Museum of
Art, where she was responsible

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.830
for the collection of American
paintings in the preparation

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:09.813
of the reopening of the American Wing.

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:13.890
Ms. Modestini was engaged by
the Samuel Kress Foundation

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:16.800
in 1983 to undertake
a comprehensive survey

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.260
of Kress paintings that had been donated

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:22.920
to 18 museums around the
United States in 1961

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.770
under the Foundation's
Regional Gallery Program.

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:29.040
In the late 1980s, she initiated
a pilot class combining

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.990
the results of her ongoing survey

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:32.820
of the disbursed Kress collection,

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:35.250
and her new role as an educator.

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:37.470
The success of the class led to the launch

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:40.620
of the Samuel H Kress Program in paintings

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:42.513
and conservation in 1989.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:45.810
The purpose of it, I want
to tell you a little bit

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:48.630
about this program because
it's incredibly unique.

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:51.150
The purpose of the Kress
Program is to provide students

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:54.480
with an opportunity to complete
the study and restoration

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:57.630
of a painting under the
guidance of Ms. Modestini.

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:00.810
Assisted by a Kress fellow, a
young professional conservator

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:03.990
in the context of an
advanced treatment class.

00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:07.170
And this is, what is in
store as a treat for those

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:09.480
of us science geeks as well who love art,

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:10.710
and are science geeks.

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:12.590
With the progress of instrumental analysis

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:14.940
in the burgeoning interest
in the study of materials

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:17.220
and techniques amongst art historians,

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:21.090
the course has expanded to
include many technical topics,

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:24.510
including the Study of
polarized light microscopy

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:27.090
and cross-sectional
analysis of paint samples

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:30.960
to identify pigments and
non-destructive identification

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.960
of their constituents
with X-ray fluorescence,

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:36.210
and SCM/EDS imaging with

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:39.720
the ultraviolet digital X-ray radiography,

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:41.820
infrared reflectography,

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:45.450
and multi-band reflectography
are also employed.

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:49.710
In May 2017, Ms. Modestini
received a Doctor

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:53.520
of Humane Letters, Honorees
Kaza from Fairfield University.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:56.310
Her book "Masterpieces"
on the "Life and Work

00:04:56.310 --> 00:05:00.450
of Mario Modestini" was published in 2018.

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:04.290
So, this introduction was
just to give you a sense

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:07.050
of the incredibly illustrious career

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:11.430
that Professor Modestini
has had, and put for you

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:13.830
in the context what you are going to see

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:17.010
in this absolutely wonderful documentary

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:21.000
where very much like a
scientist she uncovered.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:23.910
She had this moment of discovery.

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:26.850
So, I'm dying to ask her more about this

00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:30.090
when we are done with the film, but,

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:34.350
so I invite you all to
enjoy watching this film,

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:38.127
and staying for the panel
discussion afterward.

00:05:47.335 --> 00:05:51.252
Okay, so I would like to
invite Dianne and Irma

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:55.440
for the panel discussion but before that,

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:59.430
let me give you a brief
introduction to Irma,

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:03.810
who is our Susan Morse
Hilles Chief Conservator

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:06.630
at the Yale University Art Gallery.

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:10.140
And she has had a very long
and distinguished career

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:12.030
in the field of conservation.

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:16.770
She was trained at the
prestigious, I'm not gonna say it,

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:19.320
my Italian is not good enough in Florence,

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:20.250
where she worked on a

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:23.430
very important conservation
projects involving paintings

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:25.530
by Peter Paul Rubens.

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:30.000
In 2000, she came to Yale
as a guest conservator,

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.230
and then joined Yale fully in 2004.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:40.110
And she is the Chief
Conservator, and I am delighted

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:44.130
to have her to be in
conversation with Dianne today.

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:45.183
Please, welcome.

00:06:47.122 --> 00:06:50.205
(everybody applauds)

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:54.840
Now, it's something to be
seeing an image of this

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:59.220
from Dianne's laptop, (chuckles)
so it's quite a treat.

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:00.170
Thank you for that.

00:07:15.868 --> 00:07:17.070
I think the mics are working.

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:18.900
- They're working?
- Yes.

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:22.530
So, I think I'd like to
give Irma the opportunity

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:25.470
to ask a question or
two before we open it up

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:28.680
to the audience and continue, okay?

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:33.680
- So, I have a question
for you, which is, if.

00:07:34.557 --> 00:07:37.053
- We can't hear you.
- Yeah, closer to the mic.

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:40.560
- Is it on?
- Yeah, it is, oh, okay.

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:45.560
- My question to you is, if
you had found the painting

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:50.070
in a museum setting,

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:55.070
and would you have changed
your approach to conservation?

00:07:55.200 --> 00:08:00.200
And in particular to the
reconstruction of the areas

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:06.270
of losses, if you feel
like in a museum setting,

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:08.130
you would have like sort
of approached things

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:12.930
slightly differently, and if
perhaps you would have used a

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:17.930
sort of advisory committee
to help that process prior

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:21.390
to actually your restoration.

00:08:21.390 --> 00:08:24.630
- Well, it's a scenario
that didn't happen,

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:28.110
and that is very far from,
I mean, it's just coming

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:29.490
from a different direction.

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:32.280
The museum owns a Leonardo da Vinci,

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:34.680
and they decide to restore it.

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:36.960
Yes, there's a committee and all of that.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:39.240
And, you know, it's always would be nice

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:40.893
to have that kind of advice.

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:44.550
In terms of my restoration,

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:46.200
I wouldn't do anything different.

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:51.420
Yeah, there's, you can't see it.

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:56.400
You know, I created this
website and you can go on it

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:01.380
and see, there's a image,
there's an ultraviolet image

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:06.790
of the painting, which
shows my restorations

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:13.220
and the original, and you
can see that they are,

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:19.430
they can, in fact, I did an
overlay also to check myself.

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:23.220
And you can see that the retouching

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:26.460
or the end painting corresponds exactly

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:28.233
with the areas of loss.

00:09:29.790 --> 00:09:33.880
And that also, a very
important point is that

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:40.140
the areas of loss are
surrounded by passages of paint

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:45.140
that are absolutely intact,
that have all these thin layers

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:49.410
of modeling, which are,
you know, in the...

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:52.890
For the flesh tones, there
are about five different

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:54.360
very thin paint layers,

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:59.360
and had that not been the case,

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:03.480
if as we say in conservation

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:08.430
all the paint had been slightly worn away,

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:10.880
I wouldn't have been able
to do that restoration,

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:14.520
but I would've had to do
something much more neutral.

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:18.210
But because there were
these adjoining areas

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:23.210
that were so perfectly preserved,
I felt that it was even

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:28.413
almost necessary in order to, you know,

00:10:30.454 --> 00:10:33.210
to make them read properly,

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:35.613
to do the kind of restoration that I did.

00:10:40.005 --> 00:10:42.480
- I'm curious about
something else, you know,

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:45.090
I'm a scientist and we
have these little moments

00:10:45.090 --> 00:10:48.600
of discovery, you know, they
may not be shattering sometimes

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:51.960
just the joy of figuring
things out for oneself.

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:54.120
And, of course, there's
the dramatic eureka,

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:57.570
whatever that is associated
with scientific discovery.

00:10:57.570 --> 00:11:00.480
I'm curious what emotional
state you were in

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:02.820
when it dawned on you.
- Mm-hmm.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:05.940
- That moment when you thought
you saw it was a Leonardo,

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:07.530
what did it feel like?

00:11:07.530 --> 00:11:09.900
- Well, I had already, at that point,

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:13.173
I had been working on it
for quite a long time,

00:11:14.203 --> 00:11:18.187
and I knew that by that time I had all

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:23.100
the art historical information about it,

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:25.980
Martin Kemp had given his opinion, and,

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:34.250
but I was still, to myself
not entirely convinced

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:42.920
or maybe not that, I mean,
to the point where I could,

00:11:43.327 --> 00:11:46.837
you know, put my life on the line and say,

00:11:46.837 --> 00:11:51.450
"Yes, I think this is by
Leonardo, it's not by Luini,

00:11:51.450 --> 00:11:55.380
it's not by Butroffio,
it's, you know, this is...

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:58.080
This can only be by Leonardo."

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:01.470
- But did you suspect that
at the first look already?

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:05.400
- At the first look, no, I
didn't because it was, I didn't,

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:09.480
first of all this, you know, was a period

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:11.700
when my husband was very ill.

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:14.910
I actually didn't wanna work
on the picture, I tried-

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:17.077
- The prominence was a bit dodgy, right?

00:12:17.077 --> 00:12:18.930
- No, the prominence is actually not so,

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:19.763
I mean, it's not-

00:12:19.763 --> 00:12:24.390
- Where it was procured at
first resurfaced, right?

00:12:24.390 --> 00:12:26.550
In New Orleans and the sort of obscure.

00:12:26.550 --> 00:12:30.513
- Yes, yes, eventually, of
course, the Wall Street Journal.

00:12:31.588 --> 00:12:33.750
- (speakers talking over each
other) I knew that it was

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:35.430
in the Cook Collection.
- Right.

00:12:35.430 --> 00:12:37.560
- So, it's not that it
didn't have any prominence ,

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:41.130
it's just that there's no
prominence before 1900,

00:12:41.130 --> 00:12:43.080
but that's true of many, many pictures.

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:49.880
So, I didn't, you know, in the beginning,

00:12:50.310 --> 00:12:55.310
I really didn't feel that I had

00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:57.840
the kind of time or energy

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:00.810
to do it, and I was taking care of Mario,

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:05.250
and he was not really
not well at that stage.

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:07.473
And it wasn't until I showed it to him,

00:13:08.310 --> 00:13:10.413
and he reacted the way he did,

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:17.337
that I decided to take
it on because he hadn't.

00:13:20.025 --> 00:13:21.480
- No, he, I mean, it was clear,

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:23.460
he thought it was something serious worthy

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:25.470
of investigation.
- Yes, he's, in fact,

00:13:25.470 --> 00:13:27.900
he mentioned, he says
by a very great artist,

00:13:27.900 --> 00:13:29.670
degeneration after Leonardo,

00:13:29.670 --> 00:13:33.600
Mario had studied
extensively early Leonardo,

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:35.520
which he know really well.

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:38.610
So, this quite late Leonardo in a way,

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:42.570
is kind of something
like the next generation,

00:13:42.570 --> 00:13:46.080
so, it kind of eventually all makes sense.

00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:49.620
But it, the fact that
he responded to this,

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:52.530
he hadn't responded to anything, you know,

00:13:52.530 --> 00:13:55.173
for almost a year.

00:13:56.010 --> 00:13:58.500
And, you know, I would
try to show him paintings,

00:13:58.500 --> 00:14:01.620
show him catalogs, and he
would just brush them away.

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:04.353
So, this was, it was something, you know,

00:14:05.345 --> 00:14:07.270
he took it in his hands and
he looked at it, you know,

00:14:07.270 --> 00:14:09.393
for a very long time.

00:14:12.377 --> 00:14:13.740
And then he said, "You know,

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:16.807
this is by a very great
artist," and so I thought,

00:14:16.807 --> 00:14:18.897
"Wait a minute, I'm missing something."

00:14:20.610 --> 00:14:21.790
Yeah, that...

00:14:22.665 --> 00:14:23.880
- That you should look harder.

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:24.933
- Yes, yeah.

00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:28.350
- We'd like to open up

00:14:28.350 --> 00:14:30.240
but we will continue the conversation,

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:32.370
but if anyone has a pressing question

00:14:32.370 --> 00:14:36.090
and the walking mic is behind you.

00:14:36.090 --> 00:14:37.860
You have it Frederick?

00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:39.810
- [Attendee 1] I was just curious,

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:43.320
so the prominence has been
established through 1900,

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:46.260
and then I guess, a Wall Street
Journal article will tell me

00:14:46.260 --> 00:14:50.250
how it got to the Auction
House in Louisiana and selling

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:51.450
for a $1,000,

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:53.580
- Yeah, it's also on my website.

00:14:53.580 --> 00:14:56.580
- Okay, and did you know
anything about the prominence

00:14:56.580 --> 00:14:58.470
of the damage to it?

00:14:58.470 --> 00:15:00.987
Could you analyze like their splinter?

00:15:00.987 --> 00:15:04.020
So, a piece of wood went
through wood and why it,

00:15:04.020 --> 00:15:08.130
how it got from being part of this king,

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:10.770
like the trunk King Charles or whether

00:15:10.770 --> 00:15:14.340
to being something that
somebody stored somewhere.

00:15:14.340 --> 00:15:15.810
- It was a discard.
- Was vulnerable.

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:16.983
- Yeah.
- Thank you.

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:24.930
- It had a, the painting,
it had an English cradle.

00:15:24.930 --> 00:15:29.930
A cradle is a kind of a
wooden lattice that's glued

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:33.030
to the back of the panel
to keep it straight.

00:15:33.030 --> 00:15:37.083
And the type of cradle
was definitely English.

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:43.880
And I asked a panel
paintings restorer in London

00:15:46.770 --> 00:15:48.390
to confirm this, which he did.

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:52.593
And then I also asked
him if he had, because,

00:15:53.940 --> 00:15:57.630
I've seen little, you know,
where there's a slight step,

00:15:57.630 --> 00:16:00.630
I've seen a little bit of
shaving down on pictures

00:16:00.630 --> 00:16:02.670
from time to time but nothing like this,

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:05.530
because someone had clearly taken a plane

00:16:06.600 --> 00:16:10.470
and just went right through
the picture, you know,

00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:12.900
taking off, you know, original paint.

00:16:12.900 --> 00:16:16.010
It was just, it was a
handyman, you know, it was...

00:16:17.670 --> 00:16:20.643
And I asked them if they
had seen anything like this,

00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:25.900
and they said they had
seen it on Tudor portraits

00:16:27.570 --> 00:16:30.633
that from country houses.

00:16:32.430 --> 00:16:36.720
So, that was just kind
of one little clue that,

00:16:38.677 --> 00:16:43.677
you know, that may, that
it was in England in the,

00:16:45.720 --> 00:16:49.593
you know, late 18th,
early 19th century still.

00:16:54.585 --> 00:16:58.380
- I think there was a question there?

00:16:58.380 --> 00:16:59.310
- [Attendee 3] Yes, thanks.

00:16:59.310 --> 00:17:03.960
Thanks so much for
joining us this evening,

00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:07.143
and especially for sharing your insights.

00:17:10.411 --> 00:17:13.940
I'm curious to know about the thumbprint,

00:17:18.210 --> 00:17:23.210
whether it was analyzed, identified,

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:29.700
what happened to to the thumbprint?

00:17:29.700 --> 00:17:31.323
- It's not a thumbprint.

00:17:35.460 --> 00:17:37.800
It's a painted thumb.

00:17:37.800 --> 00:17:40.200
- [Attendee 2] Oh, okay, so
I thought there was a print

00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:42.330
of the artists.

00:17:42.330 --> 00:17:47.010
- No, there are some,
you know, fingerprints

00:17:47.010 --> 00:17:49.500
in some paintings by Leonardo.

00:17:49.500 --> 00:17:51.300
There are some in Ginevra.

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:54.270
There's some in the Saint
Jerome in the Vatican

00:17:54.270 --> 00:17:59.270
but there, now, this is just
what we call a pentimento.

00:18:00.180 --> 00:18:02.400
It means a change of mind.

00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:06.330
And there are other
changes in the painting,

00:18:06.330 --> 00:18:08.193
but they're all very slight.

00:18:10.050 --> 00:18:11.553
And they're,

00:18:14.730 --> 00:18:19.730
I'd wonder, I've always
wondered why he went through

00:18:20.250 --> 00:18:23.640
so much trouble to make some
of the changes that he did

00:18:23.640 --> 00:18:28.640
because it meant revising a
whole part of the painting.

00:18:30.990 --> 00:18:35.283
He moved the hand holding
the orb, he moved it.

00:18:36.210 --> 00:18:40.173
He moved the hand slightly
down and slightly to the left.

00:18:41.850 --> 00:18:46.850
And he moved the mantle so
that he could put in two pieces

00:18:46.920 --> 00:18:50.550
of black, and this caused
him to have to change

00:18:50.550 --> 00:18:55.550
the whole structure of the cost bands,

00:18:56.700 --> 00:18:58.233
those embroidered bands.

00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:03.420
And it was, I mean, it's such
a slight adjustment that,

00:19:03.420 --> 00:19:07.050
but it clearly meant a great deal to him.

00:19:07.050 --> 00:19:11.790
And this is why it's also,
it's another, I think,

00:19:11.790 --> 00:19:15.333
compelling reason for
the attribution is that,

00:19:17.400 --> 00:19:21.690
and that he worked on it over
a very long period of time.

00:19:21.690 --> 00:19:25.980
Because if you compare it to,
people often compare it to

00:19:25.980 --> 00:19:29.520
the St. John the Baptist in Louvre,

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:32.520
which was painted very quickly.

00:19:32.520 --> 00:19:35.670
It was painted practically all in one go.

00:19:35.670 --> 00:19:39.540
And it has these terrible
contraction cracks

00:19:39.540 --> 00:19:41.050
where the paint slipped

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:45.603
because the paint underneath
it wasn't sufficiently dry.

00:19:47.250 --> 00:19:51.960
And the Mona Lisa also, has
many contraction cracks.

00:19:51.960 --> 00:19:55.620
The Salvador Munich has
hardly any cracks at all

00:19:55.620 --> 00:20:00.510
of that type in the paint layer.

00:20:00.510 --> 00:20:04.260
Also, the Belle Pleione has a series

00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:07.500
of very significant drying cracks.

00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:12.500
So, it's, and from the cross
sections, you can see how thin,

00:20:15.450 --> 00:20:18.093
the thin buildup of these layers of color.

00:20:19.380 --> 00:20:24.380
So, and from what I think,
as far as I can tell

00:20:26.340 --> 00:20:30.513
from the Naples copy, which
is I think the earliest one,

00:20:32.010 --> 00:20:35.410
it was probably, he probably
began to work on this picture

00:20:36.690 --> 00:20:41.690
before 1500 in Milan, and
then, continued to work on it

00:20:42.150 --> 00:20:44.910
in Florence, in Milan, in Rome,

00:20:44.910 --> 00:20:47.130
Milan again, in Rome, and in France.

00:20:47.130 --> 00:20:48.530
I think it was a long...

00:20:49.468 --> 00:20:51.873
It had an extremely long gestation.

00:20:56.280 --> 00:20:58.080
- Oh, the mic.

00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:00.090
- [Attendee 4] Yes, I have it here.

00:21:00.090 --> 00:21:00.923
- Okay.

00:21:02.040 --> 00:21:06.330
