the world, et cetera,

00:23:23.100 --> 00:23:27.150
and I wanted to know what
makes someone as a conservator

00:23:27.150 --> 00:23:28.170
the best conservator.

00:23:28.170 --> 00:23:29.280
- Oh, that was just silly.

00:23:29.280 --> 00:23:31.047
- [Attendee] I know, but I just-

00:23:31.047 --> 00:23:33.250
- I mean they just made that up, you know,

00:23:33.250 --> 00:23:34.770
- [Attendee 5] I just thought about

00:23:34.770 --> 00:23:37.773
what makes a good conservator and I also,

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:40.920
in order to be thought of,

00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:44.220
so what would esteem a conservator?

00:23:44.220 --> 00:23:48.030
And also, I was wondering
how you felt like

00:23:48.030 --> 00:23:49.320
when you were doing the retouching,

00:23:49.320 --> 00:23:53.250
you separated yourself from
the hand of the artist,

00:23:53.250 --> 00:23:56.040
and yourself, I mean, I'm
an objects conservator

00:23:56.040 --> 00:23:59.850
and my first real knowledge of

00:23:59.850 --> 00:24:03.750
inpainting techniques are
from Irma who uses a very,

00:24:03.750 --> 00:24:07.830
a visible inpainting technique,
which I feel like you,

00:24:07.830 --> 00:24:11.190
I understand her approach in
separating her own technique

00:24:11.190 --> 00:24:12.717
from the techniques of the artist.

00:24:12.717 --> 00:24:15.030
And I was wondering what your approach was

00:24:15.030 --> 00:24:16.817
to your inpainting?

00:24:18.540 --> 00:24:19.933
- Well, I try to...

00:24:20.975 --> 00:24:25.170
I tried to imitate

00:24:25.170 --> 00:24:30.170
the entire layer structure
working in myself

00:24:30.210 --> 00:24:35.190
in very thin layers, for
example, in the flesh tones.

00:24:35.190 --> 00:24:39.810
The first, Leonardo's first
layer is a very deep hot pink.

00:24:39.810 --> 00:24:42.450
So, I under painted it with that.

00:24:42.450 --> 00:24:46.050
And then, it was just a
kind of series of, you know,

00:24:46.050 --> 00:24:49.830
very thin glazes and
stumbles, and then editing,

00:24:49.830 --> 00:24:53.970
you know, I was constantly,
you know, taking off

00:24:53.970 --> 00:24:57.120
what I had put in and sort
of paring it down a bit,

00:24:57.120 --> 00:25:00.780
and which also burnished
it until, to make sure

00:25:00.780 --> 00:25:04.780
that it hadn't gone outside
the limits, but that it was,

00:25:06.517 --> 00:25:08.700
you know, I mean, that's all actually.

00:25:08.700 --> 00:25:12.063
That's how I work always anyway, so-

00:25:13.288 --> 00:25:14.820
- [Attendee 5] I was just curious how,

00:25:14.820 --> 00:25:17.820
if you're imitating their
techniques or, I mean,

00:25:17.820 --> 00:25:21.540
it sounds like you're
imitating what was built up on

00:25:21.540 --> 00:25:26.070
rather than, you know, creating something

00:25:26.070 --> 00:25:30.363
like a visual reconstruction
rather than like a technique.

00:25:32.610 --> 00:25:34.360
- I don't understand what you mean.

00:25:35.610 --> 00:25:38.567
- [Attendee 5] Well, I'm thinking
of like how, for example,

00:25:38.567 --> 00:25:41.261
as you said, mostly
when I'm thinking about

00:25:41.261 --> 00:25:46.020
like if you look at it up close,

00:25:46.020 --> 00:25:48.570
would you be able to
tell your brush strokes

00:25:48.570 --> 00:25:50.040
from Leonardo's brush strokes?

00:25:50.040 --> 00:25:52.890
Whereas if you would
look up close at some,

00:25:52.890 --> 00:25:55.110
like something that's done.
- Yeah.

00:25:55.110 --> 00:25:55.950
- [Attendee 5] Technique, for example,

00:25:55.950 --> 00:25:58.903
I can see that they're tiny
lines, but like if I step,

00:25:58.903 --> 00:25:59.837
I really have to look through them.

00:25:59.837 --> 00:26:02.670
But if I step away, I
can, it comes together

00:26:02.670 --> 00:26:03.690
as a complete picture.

00:26:03.690 --> 00:26:05.100
- I see.
- I'm wondering.

00:26:05.100 --> 00:26:07.680
- No, you would have
to be very, very close

00:26:07.680 --> 00:26:11.130
to the picture indeed,
to be able to see them.

00:26:11.130 --> 00:26:12.476
- To see your-
- Yes.

00:26:12.476 --> 00:26:14.886
- [Attendee 5] But you can tell which?

00:26:14.886 --> 00:26:19.233
- Well, I have this ultraviolet image,

00:26:20.160 --> 00:26:22.350
which is part of the documentation.

00:26:22.350 --> 00:26:24.600
- [Attendee 5] Oh, absolutely,
I just, for the viewer

00:26:24.600 --> 00:26:27.570
to see what, when
they're interacting with-

00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:30.330
- I don't work that way, yeah.

00:26:30.330 --> 00:26:34.140
- Because this a different
style technique, right?

00:26:34.140 --> 00:26:36.780
- Yeah, it's a different
approach, and I've always,

00:26:36.780 --> 00:26:39.910
you know, it's kind of
an Italian approach.

00:26:39.910 --> 00:26:42.033
- Yeah.
- And I've always,

00:26:42.990 --> 00:26:44.790
I mean, all my training from the,

00:26:44.790 --> 00:26:48.930
you know, from the very beginning,

00:26:48.930 --> 00:26:53.520
I've always worked in
this way to try to imitate

00:26:53.520 --> 00:26:56.043
the adjacent original
as quickly as, you know,

00:26:57.952 --> 00:27:00.243
as well as possible.

00:27:01.710 --> 00:27:04.320
But there are all sorts of
different problems that come up

00:27:04.320 --> 00:27:06.420
with every picture, you know, as I said,

00:27:06.420 --> 00:27:11.420
this particular painting had
perfectly preserved passages

00:27:11.730 --> 00:27:14.883
of original next to losses,

00:27:16.800 --> 00:27:19.530
which this carpenter or
whoever worked on had planed

00:27:19.530 --> 00:27:20.959
right down to the wood.
- Mm.

00:27:20.959 --> 00:27:24.830
- So, I...

00:27:27.090 --> 00:27:31.560
You know, I could have, you know, that...

00:27:31.560 --> 00:27:33.240
So, that was the, you know, I say that was

00:27:33.240 --> 00:27:35.130
the only reason I could do it.

00:27:35.130 --> 00:27:37.320
It's not, you know, but,
and if you have, you know,

00:27:37.320 --> 00:27:39.990
different kind of picture,
the different kind of damage,

00:27:39.990 --> 00:27:42.550
something that's, you
know, extremely abraded

00:27:43.800 --> 00:27:46.620
where you sort of work, you know all

00:27:46.620 --> 00:27:49.980
to tone down little losses
all over the picture

00:27:49.980 --> 00:27:53.100
until you get something
that's more cohesive.

00:27:53.100 --> 00:27:56.940
But it's just, it changes
with every, you know,

00:27:56.940 --> 00:27:58.050
with every picture.

00:27:58.050 --> 00:28:00.637
There's not a formula
that you can just say,

00:28:00.637 --> 00:28:02.430
"Oh, this is what you do."

00:28:02.430 --> 00:28:05.340
But I mean, I have a
formula for my materials.

00:28:05.340 --> 00:28:07.980
- Right, so actually, that's
a question I have for you.

00:28:07.980 --> 00:28:11.580
When you were talking about
the sort of the five layers,

00:28:11.580 --> 00:28:15.390
is the chemical composition of
the pigments, are we able to,

00:28:15.390 --> 00:28:16.560
this is a naive question.

00:28:16.560 --> 00:28:19.860
Are we able to completely
reproduce the compositions

00:28:19.860 --> 00:28:22.473
that he might have used now?

00:28:23.520 --> 00:28:28.520
- No, because for one thing,
we don't use lead white.

00:28:29.820 --> 00:28:30.653
- Right.

00:28:31.710 --> 00:28:36.710
- And I don't know, and, of
course, it can be poisonous,

00:28:37.800 --> 00:28:42.800
but I think that I would be afraid that

00:28:44.430 --> 00:28:48.810
the medium that I use
wouldn't bind it well enough

00:28:48.810 --> 00:28:52.170
so that it wouldn't react with
the atmosphere and blacken.

00:28:52.170 --> 00:28:53.220
- Right.

00:28:53.220 --> 00:28:55.350
- So. instead-
- So, potential damage.

00:28:55.350 --> 00:28:56.457
You're very worried about that.

00:28:56.457 --> 00:29:01.253
- Mm, so instead, most
conservatives use titanium oxide,

00:29:02.310 --> 00:29:05.073
the rutile form, crystal form,

00:29:05.910 --> 00:29:07.320
which is stable.

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:09.183
It's not as opaque as lead white,

00:29:10.920 --> 00:29:13.500
and there are colors that you can't use.

00:29:13.500 --> 00:29:14.520
- Because he probably, I mean,

00:29:14.520 --> 00:29:18.630
they probably had organic
pigmentation, right, which is-

00:29:18.630 --> 00:29:22.890
- There are some colors that
faded that you, you know,

00:29:22.890 --> 00:29:23.940
that you can't use.

00:29:23.940 --> 00:29:27.390
There are some, you know, there are, yeah,

00:29:27.390 --> 00:29:31.528
certain colors that alter like, you know,

00:29:31.528 --> 00:29:33.780
realgar or prevent that, you know,

00:29:33.780 --> 00:29:38.780
so you have to substitute
materials that are stable.

00:29:39.120 --> 00:29:39.953
- Mm.
- Yeah.

00:29:42.717 --> 00:29:46.650
(speakers faintly talking over each other)

00:29:46.650 --> 00:29:47.817
- There, yeah.

00:29:49.320 --> 00:29:54.090
- [Attendee 6] So, I believe
I noticed two different frames

00:29:54.090 --> 00:29:55.881
