00:29:59.457 --> 00:30:03.690
really a fancy one, like
out of period or fake or-

00:30:03.690 --> 00:30:07.230
- Look, they did, you know, a lot of this

00:30:07.230 --> 00:30:11.910
of this filming they did,
they were reenactments.

00:30:11.910 --> 00:30:12.743
- Right.

00:30:12.743 --> 00:30:17.660
- So, the painting the frame
that was on the painting

00:30:18.570 --> 00:30:23.570
when they sold it, which is at this...

00:30:24.660 --> 00:30:26.433
It's this frame, yeah.

00:30:29.760 --> 00:30:32.110
Obviously, they didn't have anymore

00:30:33.427 --> 00:30:35.910
but Robert Simon still had

00:30:35.910 --> 00:30:40.260
the frame that had been
on it when he bought it,

00:30:40.260 --> 00:30:43.500
which was the frame that
was on it when it was

00:30:43.500 --> 00:30:45.360
in the Cook Collection,
- mm.

00:30:45.360 --> 00:30:48.480
- Because it still had
the inventory numbers

00:30:48.480 --> 00:30:51.123
of the Cook Collection on the back of it.

00:30:54.468 --> 00:30:55.440
So, that's what the frame,

00:30:55.440 --> 00:30:58.170
so that, the gold frame that you see,

00:30:58.170 --> 00:30:59.730
that's the Cook Collection frame.

00:30:59.730 --> 00:31:00.563
- Mm.

00:31:03.960 --> 00:31:07.770
So, I was curious sort of the chemistry

00:31:07.770 --> 00:31:11.400
of the reconstruction of these colors.

00:31:11.400 --> 00:31:13.920
Like, I would like to
hear a little bit more.

00:31:13.920 --> 00:31:18.920
- Oh, well, so in terms
of reconstruction of law,

00:31:19.380 --> 00:31:23.550
areas of losses, well, in,

00:31:23.550 --> 00:31:28.550
I would say in Italy we have
a tendency to use a visible

00:31:29.520 --> 00:31:32.977
inpainting technique
for loss compensation.

00:31:35.970 --> 00:31:40.970
It's a theory that was
developed in the late 1930s,

00:31:41.220 --> 00:31:45.000
early '40s by Cesare Brandi who was a,

00:31:45.000 --> 00:31:48.360
who's to be considered the
father of the Italian theory of

00:31:48.360 --> 00:31:50.760
restoration, of modern
theory of restoration

00:31:50.760 --> 00:31:55.383
where he, and the Laura Maura developed

00:31:58.320 --> 00:32:00.870
this technique that is called trateggio,

00:32:00.870 --> 00:32:04.890
where you use vertical lines,

00:32:04.890 --> 00:32:09.660
you just oppose and you
superimpose pure colors,

00:32:09.660 --> 00:32:14.280
or you mix-max of two colors,
and you create this grid.

00:32:14.280 --> 00:32:19.280
And the whole point is to
hold back the personality

00:32:19.830 --> 00:32:21.270
of the conservator.

00:32:21.270 --> 00:32:24.540
So, you're reconstructing like almost

00:32:24.540 --> 00:32:27.990
that there is like some
sort of filter between

00:32:27.990 --> 00:32:30.210
the work of art and the conservator,

00:32:30.210 --> 00:32:32.910
and that your reconstruction is visible

00:32:32.910 --> 00:32:35.820
and judgable, so that when you-

00:32:35.820 --> 00:32:40.281
- So, there's a separation
that's inbuilt as the,

00:32:40.281 --> 00:32:42.450
it pulls back that-

00:32:42.450 --> 00:32:45.240
- The gaze should be able to discern.

00:32:45.240 --> 00:32:47.190
- Yes. (speakers talking over each other)

00:32:47.190 --> 00:32:50.070
So, when you're close up,
you see the area of loss.

00:32:50.070 --> 00:32:54.360
You can recognize and judge
the work of the conservator

00:32:54.360 --> 00:32:56.430
when you're far away.

00:32:56.430 --> 00:32:59.253
So, you basically value the,

00:33:00.315 --> 00:33:03.770
you try to come to balance
the historical value

00:33:03.770 --> 00:33:08.770
of the work of art by not
imposing your own work.

00:33:09.300 --> 00:33:12.930
And then, from an aesthetic point of view,

00:33:12.930 --> 00:33:16.920
you still allow the work of
art to be appreciated because-

00:33:16.920 --> 00:33:18.450
- To be also made whole, right?

00:33:18.450 --> 00:33:21.540
I mean, part of the challenge,

00:33:21.540 --> 00:33:23.400
different ways of looking at it.

00:33:23.400 --> 00:33:25.770
- Yeah.
- Is what way do you want

00:33:25.770 --> 00:33:29.190
to make this thing whole and back to-

00:33:29.190 --> 00:33:31.440
- Yes, and how you let the artist be

00:33:31.440 --> 00:33:35.168
the predominant voice
and not the restorer.

00:33:35.168 --> 00:33:36.001
- Yeah.

00:33:36.001 --> 00:33:37.657
- And their, yeah,

00:33:37.657 --> 00:33:39.480
and their sort of various ways

00:33:39.480 --> 00:33:41.643
of thinking about that and doing it.

00:33:43.292 --> 00:33:45.180
- But I love the sort of,

00:33:45.180 --> 00:33:47.580
there's a detective aspect to all of this,

